direction: down

title: |md
  ## DSQ patterns: shared vs per-CPU dispatch
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

global: "Global DSQ (SCX_DSQ_GLOBAL)\n\nSingle queue, all CPUs pull from it.\n\nPros:\n- Simple\n- Automatic load balancing\n  (idle CPUs grab next task)\n\nCons:\n- Cache locality lost\n- Lock contention at high rate\n- No latency guarantees\n\nUsed by: scx_simple (default)" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
}

percpu: "Per-CPU DSQ (scx_bpf_create_dsq)\n\nEach CPU has its own queue.\n\nPros:\n- Cache locality\n- No lock contention\n- Per-CPU policy possible\n\nCons:\n- Manual load balancing in BPF\n- Idle CPUs starve if no work locally\n\nUsed by: scx_central, scx_flatcg" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
}

central: "Central DSQ (scx_central pattern)\n\nOne CPU acts as 'central' dispatcher;\nothers enqueue but never dispatch directly.\n\nPros:\n- Centralized policy decisions\n- Flexible (anti-thrashing,\n  latency-priority queues, ...)\n\nCons:\n- Central CPU is bottleneck\n- Cross-CPU IPI cost\n\nUsed by: scx_central" {
  style.fill: "#dbeafe"
}

global -> percpu: {style.opacity: 0}
global -> central: "scaling up" {style.stroke-dash: 4}
percpu -> central: "with central control" {style.stroke-dash: 4}
